<Page pageKey="home">
  <p class="answer">I am Jeff Richardson, Owner of {entity.legal_name} in Boise, Idaho. I want to buy one established Treasure Valley business, in Ada or Canyon County, with $1 million or more in annual revenue, preferably a B2B or commercial services company operating five or more years. If you own one and are thinking about selling, call me at {entity.contact.telephone_display}.</p>

<h2>What I am looking for</h2>
<div class="scroll-x">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr><td>Where</td><td>Ada and Canyon counties: Boise, Meridian, Nampa, Eagle, Caldwell, Kuna, Star</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Size</td><td>$1 million or more in annual revenue. No upper limit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Type</td><td>B2B and commercial services preferred</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Age</td><td>5 or more years in business</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Not buying</td><td>Restaurants, retail, franchises, startups, turnarounds, businesses whose value is mostly real estate</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>If that describes your company, the rest of this page is for you. If you are close but not sure, the <a href="/what-we-buy/">what we buy</a> page covers the edges.</p>
<h2>Who is buying?</h2>
<p>Me. My name is Jeff Richardson. I live in Boise, and {entity.legal_name} is my company. It exists to buy one good business here and own it for a long time. Not a fund, not a broker, not a firm that has to buy several companies a year. One business. I have spent several years studying Treasure Valley businesses.</p>
<p>I have an MBA from Kellogg, worked in financial analytics at ServiceLink, part of Fidelity National Financial, and did strategy consulting at EY. That background means I can read your books and get to a fair number without wasting your time. It does not mean I plan to sit in your chair on Monday morning. After close I put professional management in place: your existing team, a manager we hire, or someone you help choose. My job is to be a good owner.</p>
<h2>Why sell to one named local buyer instead of a fund or a competitor?</h2>
<p>Between 2021 and 2026, at least eight long-time Treasure Valley HVAC and plumbing firms were sold to out-of-state private equity buyers [BoiseDev, Aug 2025; Arthur Berry & Co., May 2026]. For some owners that was the right call. It is not the only option, and it is not the one I am offering.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You know who is buying.</strong> My name is on the letter and I live in Boise. You deal with me from the first call to the closing table, not an associate, and not a group that changes every quarter.</li>
  <li><strong>A fund has a model, and your company has to fit it.</strong> I have one purchase to make, so I can fit the purchase to your company.</li>
  <li><strong>A competitor often wants your customer list and your best technicians, and not much else.</strong> I want the whole business, name included, running the way it runs.</li>
  <li><strong>Big buyers usually set the terms:</strong> who runs it, how fast you leave, when you get paid. I treat those as decisions we make together.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What happens to your company after you sell?</h2>
<p>I put professional management in place: your existing team, a manager we hire, or someone you help choose. Before we sign anything, we agree on the things owners usually care most about:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Who runs the company day to day</li>
  <li>Your timeline for stepping back, whether that is months or years</li>
  <li>Which employees and key people are staying, and on what terms</li>
  <li>Whether the company keeps its name (my default is yes)</li>
  <li>Your own role after close, anywhere from a clean exit to a long advisory seat</li>
  <li>How and when you get paid, including structures that fit your tax and retirement plans</li>
</ul>
<p>None of these are promises I can make on a website. They are options, and we decide them together and put them in writing.</p>
<h2>How do you start?</h2>
<p>Call me at {entity.contact.telephone_display} or email {entity.contact.email}. The first conversation is 30 minutes, confidential, and needs no documents. If your business is not a fit, I will say so on that call and try to point you toward someone better suited.</p>
<p>If you would rather write, the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> has a form with three fields. This offer does not expire. If you are thinking about selling this year, next year, or in five years, I would like to know you. When both sides are ready, I aim for a quick, professional closing within 90 days.</p>
<p>If you are a CPA, attorney, banker, or wealth advisor with a client who might fit, the <a href="/for-advisors/">for advisors</a> page is for you.</p>
</Page>
